"THE AGREEMENT LAW. The kernel never holds a row today`s lambda does not (ka:extra is empty), and the fact types whose rows differ are at most the five ka:expected names: the instance reflection, Object Type Instance is instance of Object Type and its properly-of and most-specifically-of, and the two populations read through it, Object Type has World Assumption and Federation is compiled, which the kernel reads by their rules and does not yet read over the base in time. MEASURED, one population at a time over the base: each of the other 26 holds today`s rows, none on either side alone. A new difference fails it. It failed first with an empty chunk read as a row: 281 of the 338 fact types then differed, many holding a row today did not.",
DEF("ka:expected", K(S5(A("ObjectTypeHasWorldAssumption"), A("FederationIsCompiled"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsInstanceOfObjectType"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsProperlyOfObjectType"), A("ObjectTypeInstanceIsMostSpecificallyOfObjectType")))),
"The kernel reflects the constraints into P as the metamodel`s facts, so those five populations now hold what today`s do, with today`s ids, and two of them three constraints more: the deontic prohibition DEO:p:PredicateIsExportedFromJSPackage and its two parts, which today`s reflection drops (reflect:con_deo keeps uniqueness and mandatory only) and the paper keeps, a deontic constraint being in C_S (def:schema). The compile gives each of the three its population by a recipe over the fact type (imports.md:113-117), not by roles, so they span no role; their type and modality are the rows the kernel holds and today does not, and ka:law allows exactly those.",
"And the 341 declarations and 318 local names of the names state:localnames records beyond object types: core.md:810-825 has one row per Domain that declares a Function, and an Object Type Instance, a reading or a fact type is a Function; today`s reflection keeps the object types only.",
